UID:12noj34migikb84e4he57csq2q@google.com
CREATED:20210406T000521Z
DESCRIPTION:Protests and Photography<br><a href="https://www.eventbrite.com
 /e/protests-and-photography-tickets-149179270213?aff=ebdsoporgprofile">Prot
 ests and Photography Tickets\, Thu\, Apr 15\, 2021 at 6:00 PM | Eventbrite<
 /a><br><br><br><br><br><strong>A panel discussion on ethics and media\, foc
 using on protest and movement photography in journalism and fine arts.</str
 ong><br><br><h2>About this Event</h2><br><br><p>In Conversation: Firehouse
 Art Center curator Brandy Coons\, photographers Armando Geneyro and Nolan S
 epter\, journalist Angie Chuang\, and attorney Caroline Kert.</p><p>Join us
  as we discuss changing attitudes and practices surrounding photojournalism
  and protest in the age of social media. We'll discuss issues of ethics and
  the law in journalism\, photography\, and gallery presentation.</p><p>Jour
 nalists and photographers have long faced criticism and moral conflict in t
 heir work documenting dramatic events\, and new attitudes about responsibil
 ity and objectivity are creating new working practices and standards of rep
 resentation.</p><p>Caroline R. Kert\, Esq. is a Senior Attorney at Creative
  Legal\, LLC. She has served as a Director and President of Apogaea\, Inc.\
 , a local 501(c)(3) arts organization associated with Burning Man. She curr
 ently serves as Burning Man’s Boulder Regional Contact and as a Board Membe
 r of the Boulder Fringe Festival and was most recently named as the Colorad
 o Attorneys for the Arts’ Volunteer Lawyer of the Year.</p><p>Angie Chuang
 is an associate professor of journalism at CU Boulder whose research and te
 aching focus on race\, identity and representations of Otherness. She came
 to academia after 13 years as a national and regional award-winning newspap
 er reporter at The Oregonian\, The Hartford Courant and the Los Angeles Tim
 es. At The Oregonian\, she launched one of the first regional-newspaper bea
 ts on race and ethnicity issues\, and traveled to Afghanistan\, Vietnam and
  the post-Katrina Gulf Coast to cover stories. Her scholarly work on race\,
  media and American Otherness has been published in Journalism &amp\; Mass
 Communication Quarterly\; Journalism: Theory\, Practice and Criticism\; and
  Communication\, Culture and Critique. Her media commentary has appeared in
  The Washington Post\, Hyphen\, Huffington Post\, The Root\, Poynter Online
 \, The Seattle Times  and The Oregonian.</p><p>Armando Geneyro is a photogr
 apher based in Denver and California. Geneyro is a co-founder of Theyshootn
 \, a collective of photographers and videographers. As a group\, Theyshootn
  has hosted several visual-based events\, including Winter in America – an
 exhibit series that brings photography and philanthropy together to speak o
 n topics such as homelessness\, drug abuse\, and domestic violence from the
  viewpoint of new up-and-coming artists.</p><p>Nolan Septer studied photogr
 aphy at the Isaacson School for Communication\, Arts &amp\; Media at Colora
 do Mountain College. He has exhibited his work in numerous group shows in C
 olorado\, Minnesota\, and New York. An image from his series documenting pr
 otests in Denver following the death of George Floyd was displayed at the I
 nternational Center of Photography in New York City in their show ICP Conce
