BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Denver:20260213T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Denver:20260213T200000
DTSTAMP:20261030T045920Z
UID:9bblmem8128dnlcvaj7ivrr8ks_R20250101T190000@google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Denver:20260213T120000
CREATED:20200822T054209Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><p>Gallery Opening!!!</p><p><br></p><br>"SECONDS”
 <br>DOUG FELKLEY ARTIST IN RESIDENCY CULMINATION EXHIBIT<br>FEBRUARY 13- MA
 RCH 8\, 2026<br>ARTIST TALK: FEB 22: 3PM- 5PM<br>Reception Details<br>5pm-6
 pm- Masks required in Main and South Gallery<br>6pm-8pm- Masks required in
 South Gallery<br>(Gallery will have masks available)<br>The Firehouse Art C
 enter invites you to Doug Felkley’s<br>culminating exhibition. Felkley was
 selected as the 2025/6<br>Winter Artist in Residence for the Firehouse Arti
 st Occupied<br>Series. This program invites emerging and established artist
 s<br>working in all media to explore their practice while engaging<br>with
 the community. The artist utilizes the Firehouse South<br>Gallery as a work
 ing studio for three months before a<br>culminating solo exhibition in the
 Main Gallery.<br>The title of this exhibit\, “Seconds\,” is a reference to
 many things. Second careers\, second chances for found objects\,<br>second
 opportunities to teach\, as well as the passage of time — second by second.
 <br>Using bent wood lamination techniques\, Felkley creates sculptures usin
 g multiple layers of thin material glued<br>together. These layers are bent
  into the desired shape. Felkley then uses pressure\, created by vacuum or
 through<br>tape binding\, to finish the shape. The more layers in each lami
 nation\, the stronger the piece will be.<br>Doug Felkley is a teacher\, a s
 culptor\, and a sometimes reluctant furniture maker. After retiring from a
 decades long career as a gymnastics coach\, Felkley felt it was time to exp
 lore an entirely new second career. This desire for something new led Felkl
 ey to study fine furniture making and design at Red Rocks Community College
 . Upon completion of the program he began taking commissions for custom fur
 niture pieces\, something that he found extremely stressful. Looking for a
 new creative outlet\, Felkley began experimenting with wood focused sculptu
 re and<br>found expression for his creative ideas. Although he felt deeply
 connected to his new creative path\, Felkley missed working with students\,
  and out of nowhere an opportunity to be a middle school woodworking teache
 r showed up!<br>Now\, teaching woodworking as an art form gives Felkley an
 outlet for both of his passions\, teaching and art.<br>Seconds II- South Ga
 llery<br>Works by Jean Hultkrans and kelly j drumright<br>Curated by Doug F
 elkley<br>“Seconds II”\, curated by Winter Artist in Residence\, Doug Felkl
 ey is an exhibit that explores transformation. Rather than separating pract
 ices by medium or theme\, the gallery is arranged to allow kelly drumright'
 s sculptural assemblages and Jean Hultkrans’ collages and ikebana to face a
 nd echo one another across the room.<br>The installation emphasizes cycles—
 of use and reuse\, of knowing and not knowing\, of injury and repair—creati
 ng visual and emotional connections between two distinct practices. By plac
 ing altered medical materials beside layered images and organic forms\, the
  gallery becomes a space where vulnerability\, resilience\, and renewal coe
 xist. This shared environment invites viewers to move slowly\, notice conne
 ctions\, and experience the quiet power of second chances and second readin
 gs.<p>Music provided by Finn O'Sullivan </p><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260525T020314Z
SEQUENCE:6
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:GALLERY HOURS- opens 5pm for reception
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
